I upper limit after a week at 15 °C and at 20 °C. ","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"Q1VKmYtnpK"},{"type":"link","url":"https://github.com/CAHVIID/hpbbook/blob/main/chapters/code/adaptive_comfort.py","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"View script","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"CIl9RXid57"}],"urlSource":"https://github.com/CAHVIID/hpbbook/blob/main/chapters/code/adaptive_comfort.py","data":{"kind":"file","org":"CAHVIID","repo":"hpbbook","reference":"main","file":"chapters/code/adaptive_comfort.py","raw":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CAHVIID/hpbbook/main/chapters/code/adaptive_comfort.py"},"internal":false,"protocol":"github","key":"alRpPsMPaG"}],"key":"n96K0bj8cl"}],"key":"zYllhrpTLJ"}],"label":"fig-adaptive-comfort","identifier":"fig-adaptive-comfort","enumerator":"1","html_id":"fig-adaptive-comfort","key":"QC4MEFrcC4"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"BR18 overheating limits","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"key":"avvRYGlTol"}],"key":"UC3ienFzZu"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"BR18 limits overheating in dwellings to","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"l4b4KPqqu8"}],"key":"kAcwn5SxVe"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"100 hours above 27 °C","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"wQPQg7X4bD"}],"key":"SKMbQTvnwq"}],"key":"fEYM1EN4oQ"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"25 hours above 28 °C","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"elRdLukQsk"}],"key":"rwWduIp0d9"}],"key":"QezZxlVyhV"}],"key":"uWEfmRxGlf"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"operative temperature in a year. These limits are tailored to the thermal model used for BR18 compliance, with its fixed assumptions on weather, internal gains, occupancy and venting. Use these limits only with that model. Hours from a detailed dynamic simulation, another weather file or measurements in a real house should not be judged against 100 and 25 hours.","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"key":"TCt3ebVlau"}],"key":"EQoahnXoPB"}],"key":"zW4lKg3sAZ"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"D2.3 Cooling strategy","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"v1c4pQCqzj"}],"identifier":"d2-3-cooling-strategy","label":"D2.3 Cooling strategy","html_id":"d2-3-cooling-strategy","implicit":true,"key":"K0QXO60ydT"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cooling measures should be applied in the order below as it is cost-effective: each step is cheaper and uses less energy than the next, and each one reduces what the next has to handle. It also matches how severe the overheating problem is. The first steps handle an ordinary Danish summer, and the later ones are added as warm spells turn into heat waves.","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"key":"GXotZwirI6"}],"key":"F1Fw7VwH9a"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":58,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reduce solar gains while keeping daylight and view out.","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"key":"l53Ahy8ZQE"}],"key":"X7dHjAtKfb"},{"type":"text","value":" Solar-control glazing with a low g-value, sensible window areas and orientations, and fixed or movable external shading that still lets daylight in and gives a view out (","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"key":"dzy7YP9GXm"},{"type":"link","url":"/hpbbook/build/a3-glazing-1161dc642969fc66c9c3d4b024431657.md","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"a3-glazing.md","key":"k2ZjjODW1g"}],"urlSource":"a3-glazing.md","static":true,"protocol":"file","key":"aQbaEqRn7C"},{"type":"text","value":", ","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"key":"RMqFAMD5wP"},{"type":"link","url":"/d3-facade-design","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"D3 Façade design for daylight and low cooling demand","key":"bunBwk1aGp"}],"urlSource":"d3-facade-design.md","dataUrl":"/d3-facade-design.json","internal":true,"protocol":"file","key":"VEzgPwUwrA"},{"type":"text","value":"). Potentially an overhang could reduce peak solar gains while keeping a view out. Solar-coated glazings are cheap, without maintenance, but the lowest level of the g-value is constrained by the acceptable light transmittance.","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"key":"t6bxJ8AHed"}],"key":"PlVQXZQksM"}],"key":"iY0kRzl3no"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bypass the heat recovery","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"key":"Oscpso9aLi"}],"key":"QlTfSB6w5T"},{"type":"text","value":" and exploit the deep amplitude of the outdoor temperature in temperate climates. When the outdoor air is cooler than the indoor air, the ventilation unit must bypass the heat recovery. Otherwise a recovery unit with 85–90% efficiency heats the supply air back up with the warm exhaust air, and the heat removal drops to almost nothing. With the bypass open, the airflow can also be boosted, for example at night. It costs only a damper and a control setting.","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"key":"Q5NqGMvv6j"}],"key":"YjvIwxGxdt"}],"key":"ayl48ywPyF"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ventilative cooling.","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"aRjpMnlyFk"}],"key":"DBSeJoIWPS"},{"type":"text","value":" Large airflows through windows, hatches and skylights, exploiting cross and stack ventilation and night cooling of the thermal mass (","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"HZZPi4Zqn9"},{"type":"link","url":"/d4-ventilative-cooling","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"D4 Ventilative cooling","key":"yumhwtnQMV"}],"urlSource":"d4-ventilative-cooling.md","dataUrl":"/d4-ventilative-cooling.json","internal":true,"protocol":"file","key":"ipIZ97w3wq"},{"type":"text","value":", ","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"O4zw05xPOt"},{"type":"link","url":"/d5-window-openings","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"D5 Openings for ventilative cooling","key":"IoHT2FBTF0"}],"urlSource":"d5-window-openings.md","dataUrl":"/d5-window-openings.json","internal":true,"protocol":"file","key":"oavChE2Z5c"},{"type":"text","value":"). Louvred openings (hatches) are particularly beneficial as they can stay open independently of the weather and occupancy. The caveat to avoid is draught when the outdoor temperature is low.","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"tbJqBV8kg1"}],"key":"WXqFL3rcMg"}],"key":"VGtHX0r8pD"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ceiling fans.","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"key":"N3TRFyIlWn"}],"key":"cwvSfGQkEq"},{"type":"text","value":" Fans let occupants accept a higher temperature. Elevated air speeds of 0.5 m/s give a cooling effect of roughly 2 °C ","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"key":"ibVJgRcuru"},{"type":"citeGroup","kind":"parenthetical","children":[{"type":"cite","kind":"parenthetical","label":"raftery2020","identifier":"raftery2020","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Raftery & Douglass-Jaimes, 2020","key":"UViAPngASf"}],"enumerator":"4","key":"eXKwrNQWSW"}],"key":"nGFeMS44IY"},{"type":"text","value":" (","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"key":"rJeSRpfK4A"},{"type":"link","url":"/d6-ceiling-fans","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"D6 Ceiling fans","key":"aw4S713F7i"}],"urlSource":"d6-ceiling-fans.md","dataUrl":"/d6-ceiling-fans.json","internal":true,"protocol":"file","key":"dVtU3ap9wd"},{"type":"text","value":"). The energy they save in air-conditioning typically exceeds their own use by a factor of 10 to 100.","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"key":"gJuhlyqNIV"}],"key":"ejOfUWZCCH"}],"key":"hMfYBVDW5Z"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shutters.","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"key":"pVWxKWXemQ"}],"key":"GU3q8nRF1g"},{"type":"text","value":" In a heat wave, closing external shutters or fully opaque blinds during the day blocks nearly all solar gain. The price is the daylight and the view out, so shutters are kept for the hottest days. Shutters block the airflow through the windows, unless hatches/louvred openings are used as openings instead.","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"key":"OwZyGrE0eU"}],"key":"i1bPo4iP8q"}],"key":"NcT0DuWytg"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":58,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Active cooling.","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"WaUrqILDOA"}],"key":"RqJFecs4wL"},{"type":"text","value":" PV covers much of the midday cooling demand, but AC keeps running after sunset: in the 2026 European heat waves the grid strain and price spikes came in the early evening, around 19:00 (","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"CMWOiJPsnG"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Appendix D: Air-conditioning in Europe, summer 2026","key":"PDYZqBPHWj"}],"identifier":"app-ac-europe","label":"app-ac-europe","url":"/appendix-ac-europe","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/appendix-ac-europe.json","key":"d1wqtkZoVQ"},{"type":"text","value":"). Active cooling also forces the manual/automatic window openings to close.","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"hbt2Go8DJq"}],"key":"lQxE8yDN6g"}],"key":"F8CkvEvNGN"}],"key":"bGWViaVz2b"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Steps 1 to 4 are decided early in design, by the architecture, the openings and the ventilation system, and cost little when planned from the start.","position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"key":"aYadBg8EmA"}],"key":"HOoJ6SzxPr"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":61,"column":1},"end":{"line":61,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In a well-designed Danish house, steps 1 to 5 should be enough to ride out heat waves without active cooling.","position":{"start":{"line":61,"column":1},"end":{"line":61,"column":1}},"key":"zYjcKg95yb"}],"key":"nmeOVy4dK3"}],"key":"CwS1rFNn1s"}],"key":"iJDlRwbM6i"},"references":{"cite":{"order":["lyhne2023","sukanen2023","en16798","raftery2020"],"data":{"lyhne2023":{"label":"lyhne2023","enumerator":"1","doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-36316-0_14","html":"Lyhne, M. (2023). The Thermal Indoor Environment of Danish Detached Houses: Past, Present and Future. In <i>Design for Health</i> (pp. 183–194). Springer, Cham. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\" href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-36316-0_14\">10.1007/978-3-031-36316-0_14</a>","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-36316-0_14"},"sukanen2023":{"label":"sukanen2023","enumerator":"2","doi":"10.1177/1420326X231160977","html":"Sukanen, H., Taylor, J., Castaño-Rosa, R., Pelsmakers, S., Lehtinen, T., & Kaasalainen, T. (2023). Passive mitigation of overheating in Finnish apartments under current and future climates. <i>Indoor and Built Environment</i>, <i>32</i>(7). <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\" href=\"https://doi.org/10.1177/1420326X231160977\">10.1177/1420326X231160977</a>","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/1420326X231160977"},"en16798":{"label":"en16798","enumerator":"3","html":"CEN. (2019). <i>EN 16798-1:2019 Energy performance of buildings – Ventilation for buildings – Part 1: Indoor environmental input parameters for design and assessment of energy performance of buildings</i> [Techreport]. European Committee for Standardization."},"raftery2020":{"label":"raftery2020","enumerator":"4","html":"Raftery, P., & Douglass-Jaimes, D. (2020). <i>Ceiling Fan Design Guide</i> [Techreport]. Center for the Built Environment, University of California, Berkeley. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\" href=\"https://escholarship.org/uc/item/6s44510d\">https://escholarship.org/uc/item/6s44510d</a>","url":"https://escholarship.org/uc/item/6s44510d"}}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"D1 Future climate and weather files","short_title":"Future climate and weather files (under revision)","url":"/d1-weather-files","group":"Part D: Cooling"},"next":{"title":"D3 Façade design for daylight and low cooling demand","short_title":"Façade design (under revision)","url":"/d3-facade-design","group":"Part D: Cooling"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}